As a senior cloud solutions architect, I'd like to emphasize the importance of defense-in-depth security practices when designing multi-tenant SaaS deployments.

When it comes to infrastructure, there are often trade-offs between cost, performance, and operational complexity. For instance, using serverless functions can be more cost-effective in the short term, but it may require more operational complexity to manage and monitor.

Here's an example of how we can use Terraform to create a sample infrastructure for a multi-tenant SaaS application:

```terraform
# Configure the AWS provider
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

# Create a VPC
resource "aws_vpc" "example" {
  cidr_block = "10.0.0.0/16"
}

# Create a subnet
resource "aws_subnet" "example" {
  cidr_block = "10.0.1.0/24"
  vpc_id     = aws_vpc.example.id
  availability_zone = "us-west-2a"
}

# Create a database instance
resource "aws_db_instance" "example" {
  engine           = "postgres"
  instance_class   = "db.t2.micro"
  allocated_storage = 20
  engine_version   = "13.9"
  vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.example.id]
}

# Create a security group
resource "aws_security_group" "example" {
  name        = "example-sg"
  description = "Allow inbound traffic on port 5432"
  vpc_id      = aws_vpc.example.id

  ingress {
    from_port   = 5432
    to_port     = 5432
    protocol    = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = ["0.0.0.0/0"]
  }
}

# Create a Lambda function
resource "aws_lambda_function" "example" {
  function_name = "example-lambda"
  handler       = "index.handler"
  runtime       = "nodejs14.x"
  role          = aws_iam_role.example.arn
}

# Create an IAM role for the Lambda function
resource "aws_iam_role" "example" {
  name        = "example-iam-role"
  description = "Role for the Lambda function"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "lambda.amazonaws.com"
        }
      }
    ]
  })
}

# Create an IAM policy for the Lambda function
resource "aws_iam_policy" "example" {
  name        = "example-iam-policy"
  description = "Policy for the Lambda function"

  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = "logs:CreateLogGroup"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Resource = "arn:aws:logs:*:*:*"
      },
      {
        Action = "logs:CreateLogStream"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Resource = "arn:aws:logs:*:*:*"
      },
      {
        Action = "logs:PutLogEvents"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Resource = "arn:aws:logs:*:*:*"
      }
    ]
  })
}

# Attach the IAM policy to the IAM role
resource "aws_iam_role_policy_attachment" "example" {
  role       = aws_iam_role.example.name
  policy_arn = aws_iam_policy.example.arn
}
```

This is just a sample infrastructure, and you should adjust it according to your specific needs. However, this example demonstrates how to create a VPC, subnet, database instance, security group, Lambda function, and IAM role for a multi-tenant SaaS application.

As a reminder, defense-in-depth security practices are essential when designing infrastructure for sensitive data. Make sure to implement measures such as:

* Using a secure connection protocol (HTTPS)
* Implementing authentication and authorization mechanisms
* Using encryption at rest and in transit
* Regularly updating and patching dependencies
* Monitoring for suspicious activity

By following these best practices, you can help protect your customers' sensitive data and ensure the security of your infrastructure.

